### `bumped_spread_backtest.py`
Standalone backtest for selling credit spreads with strikes bumped 1 closer to ATM, on days where the parent strategy traded a 1-lot (hit `min_size` floor due to cheap spread price). Only trades the same side (put/call) as the parent's 1-lot trade. Now deployed to prod — see Prod Bumped Spread Strategy section below.

**Configuration (top of file):**
- `BUMP_CONFIGS` — per-strategy dict with `dollar_target` and `max_size` for each parent strategy
- `BUMP_STEP` — how far to bump short strike toward ATM (default: 5 = 1 SPXW index)
- `SPREAD_WIDTH` — distance between short and long legs (default: 15 to match prod; set to 5 for $5-wide testing)
- `MAX_ATM_VOL` — vol filter; set to `None` to disable (default: 0.14)

**How it works:** For each parent 1-lot day, creates a bumped config (short strike indices decremented by 1 within the parent's `min_strike_distance` ladder, long strike indices set to `bumped_short + SPREAD_WIDTH/STRIKE_STEP`), runs `apply_config`, then keeps only the PnL from the side that was a 1-lot in the original. Produces a cumulative PnL chart and exports to Excel.

**9/11/26 -- double-bump bug fixed; backtest now matches prod strike selection.** Before this date the bumped config also lowered `min_strike_distance` by `BUMP_STEP`. Because the distance filter defines index 0 in the index-based strike logic, on days where spot sat within $1 of a strike (parent's filter dropped the near strike, ~25% of legs) the backtest sold TWO strikes closer than the parent, while prod (`bumped_spread_trader.py`) is always parent short +/- $5. Found on 9/9/26 (prod 7635/7620 @ 0.65 vs backtest 7640/7625 @ 1.90); confirmed on 3 of 8 prod triggers in the 7/16-9/10 logs. Any `bumped_spread_backtest_*.xlsx` dated before 9/11/26 overstates bumped PnL: fixing changes 43/172 legs, combined PnL 343k -> 190k, GROWS 192k -> 119k, SHRINKS 169k -> 85k, VS_MARKET -18k -> -14k; on affected days credit falls 1.83 -> 0.47 and WR rises 74% -> 95%. `--legacy-double-bump` reproduces the old method (writes `bumped_spread_LEGACY_DOUBLE_BUMP_backtest_*.xlsx`, deliberately outside the review pack's glob). Prod bumped sizing was halved the same day: `prod_change_logs/9_11_2026.md`.

**Levered-rebal veto (9/9/26) -- modeled.** Prod blocks IMBAL_GROWS BUMPED (and CLOSER) when the projected 3:55pm levered-ETF rebal is adverse to the sold side (Calls if rebal > +T, Puts if rebal < -T). This backtest mirrors it via `REBAL_VETO_ENABLED` / `REBAL_VETO_THRESHOLD_B` (5.0B, the analysis threshold) / `REBAL_VETO_PARENTS`, with the historical rebal from `rebal_355_history.py` (vbam-data-oracle by-stock parquets, 3:55 exchange bucket). Vetoed legs are printed as `[rebal veto] ... BUMP: N leg(s) vetoed | PnL removed=$...`. **Prod runs a tighter 4.5B threshold** (deliberate buffer), so prod may veto a few legs the backtest keeps; the historical number is also the 15:50-snap pipeline while prod reads the live 15:49 estimate. See `prod_change_logs/9_9_2026.md`.

**CLOSER precedence -- two different things.** (A) Closer-vs-bumped precedence (prod skips bumped on any parent/side where the CLOSER fired, `closer_applied` in `bumped_spread_trader.py`) **is modeled**: `RESPECT_CLOSER_PRECEDENCE=True` drops those sides via `closer_precedence.closer_fired_keys()` (printed as `[closer precedence] ... N bumped leg(s) suppressed`). (B) The parent RESIZE by the closer (prod moves lots from the parent to the CLOSER, e.g. "GROWS 1068 -> 320") is **not modeled in the main parent backtest** (`EOD_OPTION_ALGOS_*.xlsx` keeps the parent at full size and also counts the closer -- double-counting the offset lots); only `closer_strike_backtest.py` computes the decremented-parent counterfactual (`parent_new_size` / `pnl_diff_vs_base`). Decompose that gap per `algo_daily_review/review_corrections.txt` correction #15. The backtest also applies TODAY's prod sizing x SIZE_KNOB to all history, so magnitudes on older dates reflect current sizing, not what was deployed at the time.

**Key finding (April 2026):** With `atmVol < 0.14` filter and $15-wide spreads, the bumped strategy looks decent since ~2024. Deployed to prod in April 2026 with $10k target / max 20 contracts.

### `closer_strike_backtest.py`
Standalone backtest for the CLOSER strike strategy (the prod-deployed companion to bumped — see Prod CLOSER Strategy section). Mirrors prod logic in `D:/PycharmProjects/scratch/algos/closer_spread_trader.py` exactly: signal "well-within" gate + parent credit ≤ `MAX_BASE_PRICE` + closest-to-uPrc strike selection in the allowed range (up to $1 ITM, up to $4.5 OTM), $5-wide spread, sizing matched to parent config.

**Configuration:**
- `CLOSER_CONFIGS` — auto-synced from prod's `CLOSER_PARENT_CONFIGS`. To override and test alternates, uncomment the MANUAL OVERRIDE block at the top of the file.
- Strike rule constants (`MAX_ITM_DIST`, `MAX_OTM_DIST`, `SPREAD_WIDTH`, `MAX_BASE_PRICE`, `FP_EPS`) imported directly from prod config so any change in prod automatically flows through.

**Levered-rebal veto (9/9/26) -- modeled:** prod blocks IMBAL_GROWS CLOSER when the projected 3:55pm levered-ETF rebal is adverse to the sold side (Calls if rebal > +T, Puts if rebal < -T). Mirrored here via `REBAL_VETO_ENABLED` / `REBAL_VETO_THRESHOLD_B` (5.0B analysis threshold; **prod runs 4.5B**) / `REBAL_VETO_PARENTS`, rebal history from `rebal_355_history.py`; the same veto is applied inside `closer_precedence.closer_fired_keys` so the bumped backtest does not credit a vetoed CLOSER with taking the day. Vetoed legs are still priced and printed (`[rebal veto] ... CLOSER: N leg(s) vetoed | closer-leg PnL removed=$... | diff-vs-base removed=$...`) but excluded from results; each row also carries `rebal_355_B`. See `prod_change_logs/9_9_2026.md`.

**Pricing/sizing nuance:**
- Backtest uses cross pricing (sell short at bid, buy long at ask) while prod sizes off SR `sprc` (closer to mid). In practice both produce the same size at typical CLOSER credits since `max_size` binds — but the backtest is slightly more pessimistic on thin-credit days.
- Backtest does not enforce a min-price-to-trigger gate. Prod inherits `min_price` from the parent strategy and passes it as an order-fill floor to `send_complex_two_waves`. Not a trigger gate either way — but the backtest will record a trade at any credit, while prod's order may sit unfilled if the cross drops below parent's `min_price`.
- Cheap-price rule (9/3/26): whenever the cross credit is <= `CHEAP_CREDIT_THRESHOLD` ($0.30) the backtest trades `CHEAP_CREDIT_SIZE` (1 lot) and sets the parent to `min_size` — the conservative model of prod's cheap-price guard (prod caps at 20 lots at the clamped $0.30 limit; the backtest assumes those floor-priced offers mostly don't fill). Historical impact: 1 of 96 closer trades (2023-11-02, -$10,640 total).

