### Limit-Fill 1-Lot Rescue Test (May 2026)

Broader re-run of the limit-order idea across ALL 350PM spread-sale algos (not just GROWS/VS_MARKET). Script: `adhoc/limit_fill_1lot_spread_sales.py`.

**What was tested:** From a fresh EOD backtest Excel, found every day a 350PM spread-sale algo got floored to 1 lot by `min_price` (credit < $0.30). For each such day, queried `t_opra_quotes` second-by-second over 15:50:10-15:54:59 and checked whether the spread's market bid (short_bid - long_ask) ever reached the limit price. If it did, assumed we instead sold the algo's `max_size` at the limit and measured incremental PnL vs the actual 1-lot trade (hypothetical replaces the 1-lot; expiry PnL at SPX settle). NDX excluded (different symbol/strike grid, OPRA not cached). Tested limits $0.30 and $0.40, and cutoff times 15:52:59 / 15:54:59 / 15:59:59.

**Results ($0.30, 15:54:59 cutoff):** Net NEGATIVE across the book (~ -$240k over 500 one-lot legs, 147 fills). Driven almost entirely by IMBAL_VS_MARKET (-$317k) -- classic adverse selection: 88% fill-win-rate but a handful of catastrophic days (one -$216k single day) sink it, because you fill max_size into a position the market is running through. Only IMBAL_GROWS was meaningfully positive (+$98k, 96% win, 1 losing day in 5+ years, every calendar year positive, robust to a $1 adverse close shift). Raising the limit to $0.40 made the aggregate WORSE (-$309k) -- fewer fills but each more adversely selected. Cutoff sensitivity is monotone: earlier cutoff = less adverse selection (15:52:59 ~ -$40k > 15:54:59 ~ -$240k > 15:59:59), because a bid that only reaches $0.30 late in the window is climbing precisely because the underlying is moving toward the short.

**Decision (not deployed):** Decided NOT to add this to prod. Only IMBAL_GROWS looked meaningfully profitable, and since all 350PM spread-sale strategies were tested at once, that single standout is plausibly just noise from multiple comparisons (researcher degrees of freedom) rather than a real, isolated edge. Not worth deploying on that basis.

### Alternative Entry Time Test (April 2026)

Tested alternative trade entry times for `ALGO_42_350PM_SPREAD_SALES_SPX_IMBAL_GROWS` (15:50:02, 15:50:07, 15:50:10, 15:50:15, 15:50:20, 15:50:25) by rebuilding `combined_dfs` with each primary OPRA fill time. Earlier entry times do not backtest better -- 15:50:02 produced ~$291k less PnL than 15:50:07 over 232 trade days. Sanity checks (same-strike credit reconciliation, 02->07 SPX drift test, outlier concentration) showed the 07 advantage is fully explained by market-maker quote tightening in the 5 seconds after the 15:50:00 imbalance print (i.e. quotes are wide at 15:50:02), not by signal quality or favorable directional drift. For now there is no strong evidence for changing the trade time away from 15:50:07. Scratchpad: `fast_version_test.py`, build scripts: `_build_time_variants.py`, `_build_regular.py`.

### Exit Triggers + First-Tick Veto Research (June 2026, 6/8/26)

Triggered by a sharp single-day loss on 6/5/26 -- explored whether two intuitive "skip / exit early" rules would have helped IMBAL_VS_MARKET and IMBAL_GROWS broadly, or whether 6/5/26 was an outlier.

**Q1: Should I automate an exit of IMBAL_VS_MARKET or IMBAL_GROWS when there is a big 355pm imbal flip?** Tested early-exit (buy back short at ask, sell long at bid -- treating NaN long-bids as $0/abandoned) at 15:55:03 across days where the 355PM imbal moved against the traded side. Tried thresholds of +/-$1B / +/-$2B / +/-$3B against the trade direction. **Answer: No.** Outside of 6/5/26 (where this would have been very helpful), broadly the data supports holding to close -- early-exit sums net out near-zero or slightly negative once the few days where the 355 imbal reverses but the close doesn't follow are included. The rule looks like noise across the full sample.

**Q2: Should I make first_tick a "no-trade" factor for either IMBAL_VS_MARKET or IMBAL_GROWS?** (i.e. require that the first tick agrees with the trade direction before firing.) Tested with both the binned `factor_first_tick` (+/-5 bps deadband) and the raw `first_tick_bps` (any nonzero sign). **Answer: No.** Same conclusion -- 6/5/26 would have benefited, but the broad sample does not support it. The first-tick sign is too noisy a signal to use as a hard veto on these algos at this sample size.

**Takeaway:** 6/5/26 was a salient single-day loss that pattern-matched to two plausible "skip" rules, but neither rule shows broad statistical support. Keep IMBAL_VS_MARKET and IMBAL_GROWS firing on the existing parent-config triggers without 355-imbal-reversal or first-tick vetoes.

**Q3 (added 2026-07-01): Should we hack CLOSER 350pm algos if there is an imbal flip such that at 355pm imbal is >$1B in the wrong-sign direction?** Re-ran the same 355-flip hack test but now on the CLOSER variants of IMBAL_VS_MARKET and IMBAL_GROWS using `hacking_355_backtest.py`. There are very few instances of this. Prior to 6/29/26, the answer was NO. Inclusive of 6/29/26, the answer would be YES. **For now, I am not automating the hack** given that I don't want to fit to what could just be a one-off situation. Revisit if more flip-day CLOSER outcomes accumulate.

**Note on CLOSER vs parent asymmetry:** The case for automating a CLOSER-specific hack looks structurally stronger than the parent case even at the same flip trigger. CLOSER trades $5-wide spreads while the $15-wide parents (`short_put_strikes:[1]`, `long_put_strikes:[4]`) have 3x the max-loss ceiling, so the spread's value at 355 does not blow up as large on CLOSER when the market has moved against the position -- hack-exit cost is capped much lower. Same signal, cheaper exit, better risk/reward on the hack for the CLOSER side.

